From: Try Omarchy contributors
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: full screen beside the camera notch

Applies after the other Try Omarchy Cocoa patches.

On a MacBook with a camera notch, AppKit's full screen puts the window below
the housing and leaves the strip beside it to the menu bar, painted black by
the window server's menu bar window (level 24) even when the menu bar is
hidden. The guest is told where the housing is (the try-omarchy package
describes it in Omarchy's display-cutouts.json, so Omarchy's own bar grows
past it), so the VM can use the whole panel:

- AppKit asks its full-screen content controller whether to reserve the
  strip for the menu bar. On a screen with a housing the answer becomes no,
  so AppKit itself gives the window the whole display, also after a Space
  switch, a display mode change or a lock. This is the one private piece:
  -[_NSFullScreenContentController reservesSpaceForMenuBarInFullScreen],
  looked up at start and checked against its signature. Without it, full
  screen stays below the housing. Public AppKit has no equivalent; a frame
  set by the app holds only until the window server tiles the Space again.
- While the window covers the strip and has the keyboard, it sits at
  NSStatusWindowLevel, just above the menu bar's black strip. An alert, a
  menu, another app taking the keyboard and the way out of full screen put
  it back at the normal level.
- Non-immersive full screen keeps macOS's auto-hidden menu bar and Dock: the
  window steps down while the pointer is at the top edge or the Dock's edge
  and comes back once it moves on past them. With macOS's "Always show the
  menu bar in full screen" on, non-immersive full screen stays below the
  housing.
- The guest is offered the whole display's size, so it follows the window.
  QEMU never sizes or moves a full-screen window on such a screen: the
  window server takes a window it moves off the strip and keeps it off,
  while AppKit still reports the whole display, so the pointer, mapped
  through AppKit's frame, would land a strip away from the picture. The
  guest's size, the scale and the level follow the window again after it
  moves, for example when its Space comes back.

Screens without a housing (external displays, Macs without a notch, the
built-in panel's modes below the camera) report no safe-area inset and keep
QEMU's behaviour. TRYOMARCHY_NOTCH=0 keeps full screen below the housing
everywhere. QEMU's log says when full screen covers the strip.

